\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Corollary}
\title{Every tower has a probability law on coherent sequences}
\date{2026-11-28}

\p{Let #{(Q_k)} be a \vocabk{tower}{fdip-01CJ}, and let #{\nu_k}
be the centered Gaussian probability law of #{Q_k} on #{F_k}.
Give the product #{\prod_{k\ge0}F_k} its product topology, and set
##{\mathcal X=\{(x_k)_{k\ge0}:x_{k-1}=T_kx_k
\text{ for every }k\ge1\}.}
There is a unique Borel probability measure on #{\mathcal X} whose
projection to #{F_k} has probability law #{\nu_k} for every #{k}.
The space #{\mathcal X} is the \vocabk{inverse limit}{tt-002D} of the field spaces with
the maps #{T_k}.}

\proof{
\p{For integers #{0\le j\le k}, let #{T_{j,k}:F_k\to F_j}
be the composite of the consecutive field maps, and put
#{T_{k,k}=\bbI}. By \ref{fdip-01CL},
#{(T_{j,k})_*\nu_k=\nu_j}. For a finite nonempty set #{J} of
depths, take #{k=\max J} and push #{\nu_k} forward by
#{x\mapsto(T_{j,k}x)_{j\in J}}. These finite joint probabilities
are consistent: passing to a larger maximum and then projecting
uses the same composite maps and the same pushforward identity.}

\p{Each #{F_k} is a finite-dimensional Euclidean Borel space.
Apply the countable projective probability construction used in
\ref{fdip-00C3} to the finite joint probabilities just specified.
It gives a unique probability measure on #{\prod_{k\ge0}F_k}
with those finite marginals. For each #{k\ge1}, the event
#{x_{k-1}=T_kx_k} is closed and has probability one, by its
specified two-coordinate marginal. The intersection of these
countably many events is #{\mathcal X}, so #{\mathcal X} is closed
and has probability one. Restriction gives the required measure.}

\p{Conversely, on #{\mathcal X} every finite group of coordinates
is determined by its largest-depth coordinate. Any probability
with the asserted single-depth marginals therefore has precisely
the finite joint probabilities above. Cylinder sets generate the
Borel sigma-algebra of this countable product and its closed
subspace, which proves uniqueness.}
}

\p{A sample #{(x_k)} is one point of this inverse limit; the Gaussian
probability is a measure on those points. The construction does not
require a pointwise field on the inverse limit of the residue sets.
The additional density conclusions and obstructions for the
fixed-ratio family remain those of \ref{fdip-00FG} and
\ref{fdip-00FL}.}
